course: biology
lesson: 154
title: More animal myths: red rags, camel humps, porcupine arrows, toads and falling cats
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Cartoons love animal tricks: a bull goes mad at a red cape, a camel unscrews its hump and pours out water, a porcupine fires quills like arrows, and Tom the cat falls off the roof and always lands neatly on his feet.
explain: Each of these has a grain of truth wrapped in a mistake: the bull really charges, the camel's hump really helps in the desert, quills really hurt, and cats really twist in the air.
explain: The trick is to ask what the animal really sees, stores or does: a bull sees red poorly and charges at the moving cape, and a camel's hump is fat, not a water tank.
explain: Playground myths work the same way: touching a toad does not give you warts, a bird will not abandon a chick you touched, and cutting a worm in half does not make two worms.
fact: Bulls are not angered by red; cattle see red poorly and charge at the cape because it moves, and in tests they charged moving capes of any colour.
fact: A camel's hump stores fat, not water; when food is short the camel uses the fat and the hump goes floppy.
fact: A thirsty camel can drink over 100 litres of water in about ten minutes, and its body loses very little water.
fact: Porcupines cannot shoot their quills; the barbed quills come out easily when touched and stick into the attacker.
fact: A North American porcupine has about 30,000 quills, and it rattles them and stamps as a warning first.
fact: Toads do not give people warts; warts are caused by a virus passed between people, but some toads have poison glands, so wash your hands after touching one.
fact: Most birds have a poor sense of smell and will not abandon a chick because a person touched it; a feathered chick hopping on the ground is often a fledgling with its parents nearby.
fact: Cutting an earthworm in half does not make two worms; sometimes the front part survives and regrows a tail, but the back part usually dies.
fact: Cats twist in the air with a righting reflex to land on their feet, but they can still be badly hurt in falls, so high open windows are a danger to them.
fact: Owls are good hunters but not cleverer than other birds; an owl's big face is mostly eyes and ears for hunting, and crows, ravens and parrots do much better in puzzle tests.
fact: The wise owl idea comes from ancient Greece, where the owl was the bird of Athena, the goddess of wisdom.
fact: Only honeybee workers usually die after stinging a person, because their barbed sting stays stuck; bumblebees and wasps can sting again and again.
fact: Hedgehogs should not be given milk, which can make them ill; leave out water and meaty cat or dog food instead.
fact: Chameleons change colour mainly to show mood, warm up or cool down and send signals, not mostly to match what they sit on, and they could never copy a tartan pattern.
exercise: Why does a bull charge at a matador's cape? | Because the cape moves | Because the cape is red | Because it hates people in hats | Because it wants to eat the cloth
exercise: What do bulls see when they look at red? | A dull colour, because cattle see red poorly | A blazing colour that makes them angry | Nothing at all, bulls are blind | Bright green, because bulls are only calm when they see green
exercise: What is inside a camel's hump? | Fat | Water | Warm air | Milk
exercise: What happens to a camel's hump when food is short? | It goes floppy as the fat is used up | It slowly fills up with water and sloshes about | It falls off | It grows bigger
exercise: How much water can a very thirsty camel drink in about ten minutes? | Over 100 litres | About one small glass | Only about one litre | About 10,000 litres
exercise: Can a porcupine shoot its quills? | No, the quills come out when touched and stick in | Yes, like arrows from a bow | Yes, but only when it sneezes | Yes, it can shoot them through the air up to ten metres
exercise: Why are porcupine quills so hard to pull out? | They have tiny backward-pointing barbs | They are glued in with poison | They twist like screws | They grow roots in the skin
exercise: What does a porcupine do before an attacker gets quilled? | Rattles its quills and stamps as a warning | Fires one sharp quill into the ground as a warning shot | Plays dead | Sings loudly
exercise: What really causes warts on people's skin? | A virus passed between people | Touching toads | Eating frogs' legs cooked in butter | Swimming in ponds
exercise: Why should you still wash your hands after touching some toads? | Some toads make poison in glands in their skin | Toads give you warts | Toads carry tiny wart seeds on their bumpy skin | Toads are always dirty
exercise: You touch a baby bird. Will its parents abandon it? | No, most birds hardly smell and do not give up their chicks | Yes, always, because the parents smell your hands and leave it | Yes, but only songbirds | Yes, unless you wash it
exercise: What usually happens when an earthworm is cut in half? | The front part may survive and regrow a tail, but the back part usually dies | Two healthy worms crawl away, one going each way | Both halves always live | Both halves grow new heads
exercise: How does a falling cat land on its feet? | It twists in the air with a righting reflex | It has springs in its paws | It flaps its ears to steer | It always falls feet first by luck, because of how cats are shaped
exercise: Which birds do best in puzzle tests? | Crows, ravens and parrots | Owls, eagles and big hawks | Ostriches, emus and turkeys | Pigeons, chickens and ducks
exercise: Where does the idea of the wise owl come from? | Ancient Greece, where the owl was Athena's bird | Owls really are the cleverest birds; tests prove it | Owls wear glasses | A scientist proved it in 1900
exercise: Which insect usually dies after stinging a person? | A honeybee worker | A bumblebee | A wasp | A hornet
exercise: Why does a chameleon mainly change colour? | To show its mood, control its warmth and send signals | To copy any pattern it sits on | Because it is ill | To make itself invisible on purpose so nothing can find it
test: Tom falls off the roof, lands on his feet and walks off whistling. What is true for real cats? | They do twist to land on their feet, but high falls can still badly hurt them | Cats can fall from any height at all without getting hurt in the slightest | Cats always land on their heads | Cats cannot twist in the air
test: A cartoon camel unscrews its hump and pours water for a thirsty traveller. What is right? | The hump holds fat; a camel copes by drinking a lot at once and losing very little water | Correct, humps are water tanks | Correct, but only in two-humped camels, which store twice as much water | The hump holds milk
test: Two camels: one with one hump, one with two. How many humps altogether? | 3 | 2 | 4 | 1
test: Three bulls run at a matador. How many horns and how many tails? | 6 horns and 3 tails | 3 horns and 6 tails | 6 horns and 6 tails | 12 horns and 3 tails
test: «Mia touched a toad, she's gonna get warts lol» What is right? | Warts come from a virus between people, not toads, but she should wash her hands | Yes, warts within a week | Yes, unless she kisses the toad straight away to break the spell | Only if it was a green toad
test: «found a baby bird on the grass, I touched it, now its mum'll dump it» What is right? | Its parents will not abandon it; a feathered fledgling is best left alone with them nearby | Yes, so take it home | Yes, so put it in a box with some bread and keep it in your room | Yes, birds hate human smell
test: «cut a worm in half, now I got two pets» What is right? | No, at best the front half survives and regrows a tail; usually the back half dies | Yes, two worms for one | Yes, and both halves grow new heads and go on living as two worms | Yes, worms do it on purpose
test: In a cartoon a chameleon sits on a tartan blanket and turns tartan. What is true? | Chameleons change colour mainly for mood, warmth and signals and cannot copy patterns like that | Chameleons copy any pattern exactly | Chameleons only change colour on blankets and cushions, never on trees | Chameleons never change colour
test: «every bee dies when it stings, so they only sting when they're really scared» What is the best correction? | Only honeybee workers usually die; bumblebees and wasps can sting again | Correct for all bees and wasps | No bee ever dies after stinging | Only wasps die after stinging, because they leave their whole sting behind
test: A cartoon hedgehog laps a saucer of milk by the back door. What should you really leave out? | Water and meaty cat or dog food | Milk, but warm | Bread soaked in milk | Chocolate biscuits
test: «owls are the cleverest birds, they've got the big wise face and the glasses» What is right? | Their big faces are mostly eyes and ears; crows and parrots do better in puzzles | Yes, owls win every bird puzzle test | Yes, real owls need glasses | Owls are the least clever of all animals, which is why they only come out at night
test: A scientist waves a still red cape and a moving blue cape at a bull. Which will the bull most likely charge? | The moving blue cape | The still bright red cape | Neither, bulls never charge | Both at the same moment
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the response of bulls to the red cape of the matador. | The bull is provoked by the movement of the cape and not by its colour | The bull is provoked exclusively by the colour red | The bull is incapable of perceiving the cape at all, being entirely blind in daylight | The bull is provoked by the hat of the matador
exam: Identify the substance principally stored within the hump of the camel. | Fat, drawn upon when food is scarce | Water, released when the animal is thirsty | Air, which renders the animal buoyant | Milk, reserved for the nourishment of the young
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the defence of the porcupine? | Barbed quills detach upon contact and lodge in the attacker, a warning being given beforehand | Quills are discharged at a distance in the manner of arrows | Quills are expelled only upon the animal sneezing | Quills contain a venom that is sprayed upon the attacker
exam: It is given that a person has handled a toad. Ascertain the consequence consistent with established fact. | The person will not thereby acquire warts, though washing of the hands is advisable | The person will acquire warts within a week | The person will acquire warts only upon the face and hands, and only after some months | The person will acquire immunity to all warts
